WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.179
This is the control room for JET,

00:00:02.179 --> 00:00:06.049
the Joint European Torus at Culham Centre
for Fusion Energy.

00:00:06.049 --> 00:00:07.899
JET is a nuclear fusion experiment,

00:00:07.899 --> 00:00:10.680
a scientific research facility that's hopefully

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:14.400
one step on the journey to plentiful, clean
fusion power.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.710
When it's running, the reactor vessel is literally

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:19.060
the hottest place in the solar system,

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:22.420
ten times hotter than the core of the sun,
and right now,

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:24.820
we are just a couple of minutes away from
an experiment --

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:27.930
or as they call it here, a pulse.

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:32.309
We're running a series of experiments at the
moment looking at disruptions.

00:00:32.309 --> 00:00:34.760
Plasmas really don't want to be there.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:38.800
When they cease to be, they create quite a
lot of mechanical energy

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:42.489
which can shake the machine, and in future
power plants

00:00:42.489 --> 00:00:45.649
we really want to try and mitigate against these problems.

00:00:45.649 --> 00:00:49.879
So right now, enormous, 700-tonne flywheels
are being spun up

00:00:49.879 --> 00:00:51.760
to provide enough energy for the experiment.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:56.719
Less than a hundredth of a gram of gas is
already in a vacuum vessel next to the torus,

00:00:56.719 --> 00:01:00.479
and when the pulse starts, it'll be pushed
in and heated:

00:01:00.479 --> 00:01:03.679
by enormous magnets, by injecting hydrogen
atoms,

00:01:03.679 --> 00:01:08.310
and just by dumping huge amounts of radio
frequency power into it,

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:10.659
making it into a superheated plasma.

00:01:10.659 --> 00:01:15.079
And then it'll hit about a hundred million
degrees Celsius,

00:01:15.079 --> 00:01:17.549
and we will start to see nuclear fusion.

00:01:17.549 --> 00:01:23.579
With fission, you're always trying to slow
it down and control that reaction.

00:01:23.579 --> 00:01:26.450
And we have exactly the opposite thing.

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:30.789
And therefore it's not possible to see a situation

00:01:30.789 --> 00:01:33.899
where you could have a large-scale accident
as you could with fission.

00:01:33.899 --> 00:01:38.359
Now, there are huge numbers of machine protection
systems watching the experiment.

00:01:38.359 --> 00:01:43.439
If anything goes wrong, power gets cut and
the whole system just stops.

00:01:43.439 --> 00:01:45.219
But if something's more subtly wrong,

00:01:45.219 --> 00:01:47.359
there's one safety feature you might not expect.

00:01:47.359 --> 00:01:50.259
Up in the ceiling are surround-sound speakers,

00:01:50.259 --> 00:01:54.030
hooked up to microphones in the reactor hall,
where no human is allowed.

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:56.579
Those speakers relay the sound from the experiment

00:01:56.579 --> 00:02:00.229
to the crew here in the control room in real
time, as if they were standing --

00:02:00.229 --> 00:02:02.819
as if this point where I'm standing right
here --

00:02:02.819 --> 00:02:04.249
was in the middle of the torus.

00:02:04.249 --> 00:02:07.450
And all the high-tech machine protection equipment
here is necessary,

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:11.760
every single bit of it. Computers can react
so much faster than us:

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:15.650
that human delay between incident and response
could be the difference

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:19.620
between a safe shutdown and some rather expensive
repair bills.

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:21.710
But if something is subtly wrong,

00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:25.200
like when you get a faint, weird noise in
your car,

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:28.900
humans might be able to notice before computers
do.

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:35.600
A couple of years ago, we had a disruption
and we could hear this clanging noise.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:40.300
And we were able to pinpoint the exact area,
and what we believed it was

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:43.130
which was a clamp which held a pipe,

00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:46.090
and we were immediately able to walk into
the area,

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:48.100
go to where we believed it was,

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:51.280
and found it -- and then could replace it.

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:54.570
The engineers here: they're taken a human
ability --

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:58.520
that feeling that's been honed by hundreds of millions
of years of evolution,

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:02.280
that something is just subtly wrong, something
is off --

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:06.310
and they've put it to work on one of the most
incredible things

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:08.910
that humanity has ever constructed.

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:11.680
All right. Here goes the pulse.

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:17.310
[Five, four, three, two, one, zero...]

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:46.650
[Translating these subtitles? Add your name here!]

